The formula in detail states that the gravitational force (\(F\)) between two objects is given by the product of the gravitational constant \(G\), the mass (\(m_1\)) of one object, the mass (\(m_2\)) of the other object, the radius of each object (\(r_{EARTH}\) and \(r_{MAN}\)), and the distance squared between the centers of the two objects (\(r^2\)).

$$F = G \frac{m_1 \times m_2 \times r_{EARTH}^2 \times r_{MAN}^2}{r^2}$$

If the man were to move halfway to the Earth's center, being at the midpoint of Earth's diameter, the radius from the center of Earth to the man would hypothetically become half of the Earth's radius (assuming the mass is evenly distributed throughout the Earth's radius). The gravitational force would then be affected by this new radius.

What would be the new gravitational force if the man moved halfway to the Earth's center (assuming the radius of the Earth is \(r_{WANT}\)):

Given that the new radius is half of the Earth's \(radius\), \(\frac{1}{2} r_{EARTH}\), we substitute into the gravitational force formula with the original symbol names (\(r_{EARTH}\) and the newly hypothetically "half-to-center” radius \(\frac{r_{WANT}}{2}\) replacing the \(r_{MAN}\) in the formula.

$$F_{NEW} = G \frac{m_1 \times m_2 \times (\frac{r_{WANT}}{2})^2 \times r_{EARTH}^2}{r^2}$$

Simplify the square of \(\frac{r_{WANT}}{2}\) to get:
$$\left(\frac{r_{WANT}}{2}\right)^2 = \frac{r_{WANT}^2}{4}$$

Hence, 
$$F_{NEW} = G \frac{m_1 \times m_2 \times \frac{r_{WANT}^2}{4} \times r_{EARTH}^2}{r^2}$$

This simplifies to:
$$F_{NEW} = G \frac{\frac{m_1 \times m_2 \times r_{WANT}^2 \times r_{EARTH}^2}{4}}{r^2}$$

or 
$$F_{NEW} = \frac{1}{4} \, G \frac{m_1 \times m_2 \times r_{EARTH}^2 \times r_{WANT}^2}{r^2}$$

Therefore, the magnitude of the gravitational force would be \(\frac{1}{4}\)th (one-fourth) the strength of gravity's original force at the surface of the Earth.

What quantitative effect does moving halfway to the center of the Earth have on the gravitational force felt by a 65 kg man according to Newton's Law of Gravitation? In effect, moving halfway to the Earth's center (assuming the Earth's radius \(r_{EARTH}\) is \(6.371 \times 10^6\) meters), the change in force would be calculated by the force ratio which is \(\frac{1}{4}\)due to the radius reduction, given gravitational force was kept proportional and constant in principle near Earth's surface under typical circumstances.


Given the original gravitational force on a 65 kg man at the Earth's surface:
$$ F_{surface} = 650 \, \text{newtons} $$

Following the dictation of Newton's Law of Gravitation:

If \(F_{surface} \propto \frac{1}{r^2}\) (indicating gravity surface acting at the Earth's radius \(r_{EARTH}^2\)),
then, moving halfway to the center:
$$ F_{half-to-center} = \frac{1}{4} \, F_{surface} $$

Since \(F_{surface} = 650 \, \text{newtons}\):
$$ F_{half-to-center} = \frac{1}{4} \times 650 \, \text{newtons} $$
$$ F_{half-to-center} = 162.5 \, \text{newtons} $$

Thus, the gravitational force felt by the 65 kg man would be 162.5 newtons if he is exactly halfway to the center of the Earth.

